{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Export /srv/nfs/shared from node1 as an NFS share accessible by node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1 shows an active, enabled NFS server exporting /srv/nfs/shared read-write specifically to node2 at 10.100.138.11; the export remained active after node1 restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1c33f6d32a204ccd","cmd-d82164d36832460e","cmd-2dc19a9e0bb9426d"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Mount the node1 share at /mnt/shared on node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"node2 directly shows the node1 export mounted at /mnt/shared over NFSv4. The fstab entry and automatic remount were also verified after node2 restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f7a6618439554df2","cmd-e1e593f49afc4210"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Use the dataops identity with UID and GID 4500 on both nodes, with the shared directory owned by dataops:dataops and mode 2770.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes report dataops UID/GID 4500. The server directory and its client-mounted view report dataops:dataops, numeric ownership 4500:4500, and mode 2770 before and after restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1c33f6d32a204ccd","cmd-13667956db474a7c","cmd-d82164d36832460e","cmd-e1e593f49afc4210"]},{"id":"req-4","requirement":"Files created from either node retain consistent ownership and permissions across the share.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Files created as dataops independently on node1 and node2 were visible from both systems as dataops:dataops with mode 660 and correct contents. A further post-client-restart write was observed correctly on node1.","evidence_ids":["cmd-028141f5d82845e7","cmd-b6c4f5e19ac84f6c","cmd-f559ed45037e43fc","cmd-e1e593f49afc4210","cmd-2dc19a9e0bb9426d"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.98,"summary":"The implementation was narrowly scoped, package state remained healthy, and no failed services or abandoned temporary files were observed. Two unnecessary initial unprivileged apt attempts created or truncated temporary apt log files but could not acquire package locks; the logs were subsequently overwritten by the successful privileged installation attempts and were absent after restart. These were bounded, low-risk, fully cleared mutations. All other observed mutations supported installation, identity/export/mount configuration, bounded behavioral evidence, or the mandatory restart protocol.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d5ab91b508e0422c","cmd-37e2d64bac3946d4","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct final-state and cross-node behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol also passed on both nodes: each had a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged restart scheduling, a changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service, mount, identities, permissions, and shared data.","limitations":["The restart acknowledgements show scheduled reboots rather than an immediate provider API acknowledgement, but subsequent changed boot identities and recovered states directly demonstrate that both restarts occurred."]}
